<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ercole Consalvi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1757–1824</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Cardinal Consalvi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ercole Consalvi was an Italian cardinal and cardinal secretary of state to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vii">Pope Pius VII</a>. He negotiated the Concordat of 1801 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815, recovered almost all the Papal States for the pope. He then reformed their administration and laws on modern lines, though many of his reforms were reversed after his retirement in 1823.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ercole-consalvi.jpg" alt="Ercole Consalvi" width="320" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Lawrence · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Lawrence_(1769-1830)_-_Ercole,_Cardinal_Consalvi_(1757-1824)_-_RCIN_404940_-_Royal_Collection.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
